course: dictionary
lesson: 3396
title: Experience: what you learn by doing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word experience can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, experience is knowledge or skill you get by doing or seeing things over time. «Older people can surprise you, because they bring skills and experience from their whole lives.» Here, experience means the skill that grows from many years of doing things.
explain: An experience can also be one thing that happens to you, especially if it leaves a strong feeling. «A good experience» or «an amazing experience» tells you how it felt. A trip to the seaside that you remember for years is an experience.
explain: As a verb, to experience something means to have it happen to you or to feel it. «Everyone gets older, but so do experience and wisdom» shows that experience can grow as we live our lives. Ask yourself: is this about skill over time, one event, or feeling something? Then test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Experience means knowledge or skill you get by doing or seeing things over time.
fact: An experience is something that happens to you, often one that leaves a strong feeling.
fact: To experience something means to have it happen to you or to feel it.
fact: Useful phrases: «from experience», «learn by experience», «life experience».
fact: When experience means knowledge, it has no plural: we say «a lot of experience», not «many experiences».
exercise: «Older people can surprise you, because they bring skills and experience from their whole lives.» In this sentence, what does experience mean? | A fun day out | A short, sharp fright | To shout loudly | *Knowledge and skill from doing things over time
exercise: Mia says, «Our school trip to the science museum was an amazing experience.» What does experience mean here? | To be sure of something | A test for a science class | Skill gained over many years | *One event that happened to you and left a strong feeling
exercise: «We will experience a loud thunderstorm tonight.» What does experience mean here? | To read about the weather | To know a lot about storms | *To have something happen to you and feel it | An old, grey wall
test: «We often learn by experience, from trying things ourselves.» What does «learn by experience» mean? | Learn by sleeping | *Learn by doing things yourself | Learn only by reading a dictionary | Learn by never trying anything new
test: Ryan writes about a personal experience for his writing lesson. What does experience mean here? | Tiredness after a long walk | A petrol station | A job advert asking for skills | *Something that happened to him, which he can describe
exam: In «Her experience of working with children made her a great teacher», what does experience mean? | Being very tired | *Skill and knowledge gained over time | To watch something on television | A single shock or fright
